{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q024_fibronectin_fragment_selection","answer":"Verified SCOPe 2.08 facts. Both domains sit in the same family, Fibronectin type III b.1.2.1 (sunid 49266), under superfamily b.1.2 (49265), fold b.1 Immunoglobulin-like beta-sandwich (48725), class b. d1uc6a1 (sunid 107766) is 1uc6 A:6-109, protein 'Ciliary neurotrophic factor receptor alpha', human; it carries no annotations, inconsistencies, or heterogeneity flags. d1o5dt2 (sunid 103884) is 1o5d T:107-205, protein 'Extracellular region of human tissue factor', human; its annotations record the comment 'complexed with cr9' and the domain-scoped, non-inherited inconsistency 'fr': fragment, missing more than one-third of the common structure and/or sequence. The observed ATOM sequence of d1o5dt2 contains only 50 residues over a nominal 99-residue range, versus 104 observed residues for d1uc6a1, which concretely confirms the fragment flag. The 1o5d entry is an X-ray structure (2.05 Å) of an Ala190 uPA protease complex; chain T also carries the sibling domain d1o5dt1 (T:6-106, b.1.2.1), which is the other tissue-factor FnIII module. Quality records: 1o5d AEROSPACI 0.287 (X-ray, 2.05 Å) versus 1uc6 AEROSPACI 0.022 (NMR, no resolution or R factor; effective resolution 4 Å).

What the fragment annotation does and does not change. (1) Homology: the fr flag does not weaken the family-level relationship. SCOPe places both domains in b.1.2.1, so they are asserted close homologs (family > superfamily > fold in evolutionary strength). Homology for d1o5dt2 is implied by level with no homology warnings. The caveat constrains how the domain can be used, not whether it is related. (2) Alignment: a structure or sequence alignment of d1uc6a1 against d1o5dt2 is only meaningful over the roughly half of the FnIII beta-sandwich that was actually observed. Coverage, RMSD, aligned length, and FATCAT/BLAST scores will be depressed for reasons that have nothing to do with divergence, so a weak score against the fragment must not be read as evidence of distant relationship, and a residue mapping should be anchored on the full-length partner. Use the SEQRES or the intact sibling d1o5dt1 (or another complete tissue-factor FnIII structure) rather than the fragment ATOM record when a complete FnIII template is required. Do not use d1o5dt2 as a modeling template for the missing strands. (3) Representative selection: AEROSPACI alone would favor 1o5d over the NMR entry 1uc6 by a wide margin, but AEROSPACI is an entry-level technical-quality score, not a completeness score, and the skill's rule is that fragments must be excluded or stratified. For a family or superfamily representative, a nonredundant ASTRAL subset, a benchmark positive, or a structural template, d1o5dt2 should be excluded (or placed in a fragment stratum) despite its better crystallographic quality; d1uc6a1 is complete but is an NMR model with low SPACI, so a representative chosen purely on quality should be drawn from the full set of complete b.1.2.1 domains, not from this pair. The two entries also differ in experimental method and in bound-complex state (1o5d is a protease–inhibitor complex), which the quality fields do not capture. (4) Family diversity: d1o5dt2 should not be counted as an independent, full sample of FnIII structural variation. Its apparent deviations from the family core (absent strands, exposed surfaces) are missing-density artifacts, not evolutionary diversity; including it would inflate estimates of structural variability and bias loop/strand-presence statistics. Diversity of the b.1.2.1 family should be assessed on complete domains, with fragments stratified or dropped and the caveat preserved in any dataset derived from release 2.08. The tissue-factor FnIII lineage is still represented in the family through the complete d1o5dt1, so dropping d1o5dt2 does not remove that protein from the family.

Limits. The fragment flag refers to the SCOPe 2.08 record; it says nothing about function, and SCOPe family placement does not establish ligand binding, receptor signaling, or interchangeability of CNTF receptor alpha and tissue factor modules. No alignment was computed here, so quantitative alignment coverage between the two domains was not verified. Quality comparison values are entry-level and nullable (1uc6 has no resolution/R factor), so no overall 'best' structure is asserted.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","mcp__scope__compare_scop_domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","mcp__scope__compare_structure_quality","mcp__scope__get_scop_sequence","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1uc6a1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1uc6a1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1uc6a1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1uc6a1/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1o5dt2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1o5dt2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1o5dt2/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1o5dt2/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1uc6/quality","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1o5d/quality","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d1o5dt2?release=2.08&source=atom&style=genetic&format=json","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d1uc6a1?release=2.08&source=atom&style=genetic&format=json","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1o5d","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1o5d/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1o5d/chains"]}]}
